;This is a sample ".OS2" file.  You'll note that every item on the left of the
;equals sign is spelled *exactly* like you see in the DOS_Settings screen when
;you right click on a DOS object and go into the session settings.

;When you create a .OS2 file, it needs to either be called PCBDOS.OS2 (which is
;used as a default for all of your DOS shells, unless an application-specifc
;.OS2 file is found), or else you need to define an application-specific .OS2
;by giving it the same name as the application (i.e. to run MYDOOR with a
;specific .OS2 file, you would create a file called MYDOOR.OS2 and put it in
;the same subdirectory where the MYDOOR file is stored).

;This sample may not work for you unless you customize the DOS_DEVICE= lines
;below because these lines have drive and path information in them which may
;not correspond with your own configuration.

;NOTE:  The "size=0" parameter is a method of loading a device driver high in
;       OS/2 and works both here as well as in the DOS_Settings screen.  To
;       use it, however, you *must* have DOS_UMB=1 (i.e. you must tell OS/2
;       that you want to have Upper Memory Blocks in which to load the driver).

DOS_FILES=20
DOS_HIGH=1
DOS_UMB=1
DOS_DEVICE=size=0 E:\SIO\VX00.SYS
DOS_DEVICE=size=0 E:\OS2\MDOS\ANSI.SYS
EMS_MEMORY_LIMIT=1024
IDLE_SECONDS=0
IDLE_SENSITIVITY=30
